    "knowledge_point": "Expressing a Quotient of Complex Numbers in Polar Form",
    "problem": "Express $z=\\frac{4}{1+2\\text{i}}$ in the form $r(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ).$ Give $\\theta $ in radians to three decimal places.",
    "worked_solution": "First, we express the given complex number in the form $z=x+y\\text{i}.$ To do this, we remove $\\text{i}$ from the denominator by multiplying the numerator and denominator of $z$ by the complex conjugate of the denominator, which is $1-2\\text{i}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =\\frac{4}{1+2\\text{i}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4(1-2\\text{i})}{(1+2\\text{i})(1-2\\text{i})} \\\\  & =\\frac{4(1-2\\text{i})}{{1}^{2}-(2\\text{i})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4(1-2\\text{i})}{1+4} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{5}-\\frac{8}{5}\\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn polar form, $r$ represents the magnitude $|z|.$ For a complex number $z=x+y\\text{i},$ the magnitude of $z$ is given by\n\n$$\n|z|=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nSo, for $z=\\frac{4}{5}-\\frac{8}{5}\\text{i},$ the magnitude is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=|z| & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{4}{5})}^{2}+{(-\\frac{8}{5})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{{4}^{2}+{8}^{2}}{{5}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{80}{25}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{16\\cdot 5}{25}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\sqrt{5}}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn polar form, $\\theta $  represents the argument $\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z).$ Since $x=\\frac{4}{5}$ is positive and $y=-\\frac{8}{5}$ is negative, $z$ lies in the fourth quadrant. So, we can find $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\theta =\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z) & =2\\pi -\\operatorname{arctan}⁡|\\frac{y}{x}| \\\\  & =2\\pi -\\operatorname{arctan}⁡|\\frac{(-\\frac{8}{5})}{(\\frac{4}{5})}| \\\\  & =2\\pi -\\operatorname{arctan}⁡2 \\\\  & ≈5.176,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places. \n\nFinally, we write the complex number $z$ in polar form:\n\n$$\nz=\\frac{4\\sqrt{5}}{5}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5.176)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5.176)).\n$$",
